           title="Curb Cut" cache="true" expires="300">
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/>
  <meta name="description" content="Find out what could make work easier, and ask for it. No form, no login, and no need to say what condition you have."/>
  <title>Curb Cut</title>
</head>
<body>
<c:CurbCutShell pageTitle="Curb Cut" current="home"
  eyebrow="Accommodation without disclosure"
  heading="Find out what could make work easier. Then ask for it."
  lede="No form. No login. And you never have to say what condition you have.">

  <div class="cta">
    <a class="btn primary" href="/ask">Ask now <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    <a class="btn" href="#doors">Other ways to reach it</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cta-note">
    You can stop at any point. Nothing is written down while you are anonymous,
    and nothing reaches your employer unless you say yes first.
  </p>

  <section>
    <div class="plain">
      <span class="tag">In one sentence</span>
      <p>
        Say what is hard at work and get real answers about what you could ask
        for &mdash; anonymously, if you want. If you decide to ask for something,
        a request is written for you and read back, and nothing is sent until
        you agree.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="sec-label">The number this is built on</p>
    <h2>Sixty-one employers in a hundred paid nothing</h2>
    <p>
      The Job Accommodation Network surveyed 26,028 employers who had contacted
      it for accommodation guidance between January 2019 and December 2024.
      5,406 replied, and 1,425 of those gave actual cost figures. This is what
      those 1,425 said.
    </p>

    <div class="tally">
      <div class="tally-fig">
        <p class="bignum">61<small>of every 100 of them paid nothing at all</small></p>
        <div class="dots" role="img"
             aria-label="A grid of 100 squares. 61 are filled in, representing the 61 percent of the 1,425 employers who gave cost figures and reported no cost at all. 39 are empty: 33 reported a one-time cost with a median of about 300 dollars, and 6 reported an ongoing cost with a median of about 2,400 dollars a year.">
          <span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span>
          <span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span>
          <span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span><span class="on"></span>
          <span class="on"></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span>
          <span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="tally-key">
        <span><i class="on"></i> Cost the employer nothing</span>
        <span><i></i> Cost something: 33 one-time, median $300; 6 ongoing, median $2,400 a year.</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Two thirds of employers called the adjustment very or extremely effective.
      <strong>Twelve per cent said it did not work</strong>, and that belongs on
      this page too, because a page that quotes only the two thirds is selling
      something. Everyone in this survey had already contacted JAN for help, so
      these are employers who were already trying.
    </p>
    <p>
      And yet in 2022, Statistics Canada found that
      <strong>more than a third of employed Canadians with a disability had an
      accommodation need that went unmet</strong>. The most common thing people
      needed was not equipment. It was modified working hours &mdash; a change
      that costs nothing and requires only that somebody say yes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="sec-label">The gap</p>
    <h2>Thirty in a hundred have one. Three tell anyone.</h2>
    <p>
      Among college-educated people in white-collar jobs in the United States,
      <strong>30% have a disability</strong> under the federal definition.
      <strong>3.2% disclose it to their employer.</strong> And of the people who
      did disclose, <strong>83% said it got them better support</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Disclosure works for five out of six people who try it, and almost nobody
      tries. That is not a population weighing costs and benefits. That is a
      door nobody can get through.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn" href="/why">Why this matters more in 2026 <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="thesis">The money was never the barrier. <em>The process is.</em></p>
    <p class="thesis-sub">
      To get help you first have to find the policy, learn its words, hold a work
      login, fill in a form, and tell a stranger something you may not want to
      tell anyone. That is a lot to ask of someone who is already having a hard
      week. The request process for a disability accommodation is, itself, an
      accessibility barrier.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="doors">
    <p class="sec-label">Four front doors</p>
    <h2>Reach it however you are able to</h2>
    <p>
      These are four doors into the same assistant, not a main route with
      fallbacks bolted on. You get an answer back the same way you asked.
      Status below is the live truth, not a plan.
    </p>

    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card open">
        <span class="chip live">Open now</span>
        <h3>This website</h3>
        <p>
          Type what is hard, read what you could ask for, approve a request or
          close the tab. No account, and nothing kept unless you ask for it.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/ask">Go to the ask page &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="card pending">
        <span class="chip wait">Awaiting carrier approval</span>
        <h3>Text message</h3>
        <p>
          Text <span class="kw">+1 276 495 9311</span> and say what is hard. No
          app to install, and it works on a phone that cannot run anything else.
          Registration with the mobile carriers is under review.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card pending">
        <span class="chip wait">Awaiting carrier approval</span>
        <h3>Voice</h3>
        <p>
          Call the same number and speak instead of typing. For anyone for whom
          typing is the hard part &mdash; tremor, pain, low vision, or a hand
          that is simply not co-operating today.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card open">
        <span class="chip live">Open now</span>
        <h3>Email</h3>
        <p>
          Write in your own time, in your own words, with no one waiting on the
          other end of a cursor. If a reply cannot be delivered, the message is
          handed to a person rather than dropped.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <p>
        <strong>Sign language goes to a human being.</strong> Signed video is
        never machine translated. It is passed to a person, and you get a text
        straight away so you are not left waiting in silence, wondering whether
        it arrived.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="sec-label">What happens</p>
    <h2>Five steps, and you can stop after any of them</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>You say what is hard</h3>
          <p>
            In your own words. &ldquo;I cannot type for long.&rdquo; &ldquo;The
            open office is unbearable by two o'clock.&rdquo; You are never asked
            why, and there is nowhere to record why.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>You get real options</h3>
          <p>
            Drawn from a sourced library of adjustments, with what each one
            typically costs. If there is nothing good to say, you are told that
            rather than given a guess.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>You decide whether to ask at all</h3>
          <p>
            Knowing is allowed. Asking is optional. Plenty of people only ever
            want to find out what exists, and that is a complete use of this.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>A request is written and read back to you</h3>
          <p>
            In your words, describing what would help &mdash; never a condition.
            You can change it, or throw it away.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>Nothing is sent until you clearly agree</h3>
          <p>
            &ldquo;I guess so&rdquo; is not agreement. A hedge gets you asked
            again, not a sent request.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="sec-label">Guarantees, not intentions</p>
    <h2>What cannot happen here</h2>
    <p>
      Each of these is prevented by the way the system is built, not by a
      promise to behave. The difference matters, because a promise can be
      quietly revised.
    </p>
    <ul class="negs">
      <li>
        <b>Your diagnosis cannot be stored, because there is nowhere to put it.</b>
        There is no field for a diagnosis, condition, disability type, medical
        note, severity or prognosis anywhere in the system. Not encrypted &mdash;
        absent. An automated check fails the build if anyone adds one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>A manager cannot be told what you have.</b> Not because access is
        restricted, but because there is genuinely nothing to tell them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Nothing can be sent to your employer without your explicit yes.</b>
        The code that creates a request refuses to run without it, and a test
        proves the refusal.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>You cannot be told to phone someone.</b> Every workplace process
        seems to end at &ldquo;give us a call.&rdquo; If you cannot use a phone,
        that is a locked door. A person here always reaches you by message.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>You cannot be asked why you turned something off.</b> Withdrawing a
        standing preference takes no reason, because the system has nowhere to
        record one, so nobody can ask you for one later.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>You cannot be profiled.</b> Nothing about you is inferred, scored or
        predicted. Every signal in the system is one you set, in your own words.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="sec-label">Checkable</p>
    <h2>Every promise on this page is enforced somewhere</h2>
    <p>
      A commitment that lives only in a policy document is an intention, and
      intentions are not load bearing. These run before any change ships. Every
      figure on this site is recorded with its source, its date and its
      denominator in <span class="kw">docs/EVIDENCE.md</span>, including the
      ones that weaken the argument.
    </p>
    <div class="proof">
      <div><b>126</b><span>structural checks that must hold before anything ships</span></div>
      <div><b>45</b><span>automated tests against the rules above</span></div>
      <div><b>0</b><span>of the 46 fields in the system hold anything medical</span></div>
      <div><b>10</b><span>adversarial scenarios the assistant is scored against</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="sec-label">Prior art</p>
    <h2>This is not a new idea. It is an unfinished one.</h2>
    <p>
      The Government of Canada already built this principle. Its Workplace
      Accessibility Passport records what someone needs once, belongs to the
      employee rather than the employer, and moves with them across the federal
      public service, so nobody has to justify themselves again to each new
      manager.
    </p>
    <p>
      It works. It is also only available if you happen to work for the federal
      public service. Curb Cut takes the same principle to everyone else &mdash;
      including contractors, and applicants who do not have a work account at all
      and are currently expected to disclose a disability to a stranger before
      they have even been hired.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="sec-label">Where the name comes from</p>
    <h2>The ramp cut into the kerb was fought for by wheelchair users</h2>
    <p>
      It is now used by anyone pushing a pram, dragging a suitcase, riding a
      bike, or walking with a knee that hurts. Nobody thinks of it as
      accommodation any more. It is just how the pavement works.
    </p>
    <p>
      A process that lets someone ask for what they need without first
      explaining their body to a stranger is the same shape of thing. Built
      because some people cannot get up the kerb, and better for everyone once
      it is there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>If you want to try it</h2>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/ask">Start on this page <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    </div>
    <p>
      Or text <span class="kw">+1 276 495 9311</span> and say what is hard, once
      carrier registration clears. That is the whole thing.
    </p>
    <p class="cta-note">
      Message and data rates may apply. Reply <span class="kw">HELP</span> for
      help, or <span class="kw">STOP</span> to stop receiving messages.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>This page, too</h2>
    <p>
      A site arguing that process shuts people out should not shut anyone out.
      Text size and contrast controls are at the top and remember your choice.
      Nothing here relies on colour alone to tell you something &mdash; every
      state also carries a word or a shape. Body text is set for AAA contrast
      rather than AA. It works by keyboard, it works with a screen reader, and
      it holds still if you have asked your device to reduce motion.
    </p>
  </section>

</c:CurbCutShell>
</body>
</html>
